.TH LOGOUT 8
.SH NAME
logout \- end a user session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logout
[
.I username
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logout
ends a login session by sending SIGHUP to the login shell's process group.
.PP
Without arguments,
.B logout
sends SIGHUP to the current process group, terminating the shell, its
foreground and background jobs, and the
.B logout
process itself.  It is the equivalent of logging off the current terminal.
.PP
With
.I username
.IR ,
which requires root privileges,
.B logout
finds the named user's login shell (the process whose user id matches and
whose process id equals its process group id) and sends SIGHUP to that
process group.  This logs the user off from another session, for example to
clear a stuck or abandoned login.
.PP
Processes that have detached from their session by calling
.BR setsid ()
are not affected, so daemons such as
.BR ftpd (8)
or
.BR httpd (8)
survive a logout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 15
.I username
Log the named user off.  Only root may do this.  If the user is not
currently logged in, an error message is printed and a non-zero status is
returned.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.I 0
The session was ended successfully.
.TP
.I 1
An error occurred: usage error, insufficient privilege, unknown user, the
user is not logged in, or the kernel task table could not be read.
.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP
.B /dev/kmem
Kernel memory, read to locate login shells.
.TP
.B /etc/passwd
User database, consulted to resolve the user name to a user id.
.PD
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR login (8),
.BR getty (8),
.BR init (8).
